Unions
What is a Union?
A union is a data type where all members share the same memory location.
union data {
  int i;
  float f;
  char c;
};
Size of union = size of largest member
Structure vs Union Comparison
Feature	Structure	Union
Memory	Separate memory for each member	Shared memory location
Size	Sum of all members + padding	Size of largest member
Usage	All members accessible at once	Only one member at a time
Efficiency	More memory usage	Memory saving
Union sizeof Example
union test {
  int x;
  char arr[8];
  int y;
};
int main() {
  printf("%d", sizeof(union test));
  return 0;
}
output
8 (largest member = char arr[8])
